[   84.698] [1;97mWhat went wrong[0m
[   84.698] Immucore checks the OS image against the sha256 recorded for it
[   84.699] before it mounts it, and the check did not pass.
[   84.699] 
[   84.699]   Problem:
[   84.699]     the check ran out of time
[   84.699]   Image:
[   84.700]     /sysroot/run/initramfs/cos-state/cOS/active.img
[   84.700]   Expected sha256 (stored in active.img.sha256):
[   84.700]     815a1742f6fb57754bd13bad0a1576fada27589f941b0c3397a90cafced92742
[   84.701]   Actual sha256 (computed from the image just now):
[   84.701]     (not available)
[   84.701] 
[   84.702]   Hashing stopped after 1m0.107s (932184064 bytes read) because image-checksum.verify-timeout
[   84.702]   is 1m0s, so the image was not verified. require is on, so it counts as a failure.
[   84.703] 
[   84.703]   Action: fail (from image-checksum.conf on the state partition)
[   84.704]   A missing or stale checksum is a problem too: require is on
[   84.704]   (from image-checksum.conf on the state partition)
[   84.704] 
[   84.704] [1;97mHow to fix[0m
[   84.705]   - Reboot and pick the passive or recovery entry in the boot menu.
[   84.705]     Then repair the image with kairos-agent upgrade or reset.
[   84.706]   - To boot this image anyway, edit the boot entry (press e) and add
[   84.706]     image-checksum.verify=warn to the kernel line.
[   84.707]   - A disk that corrupts images can corrupt more: check its health.
[   84.707] 
[   84.707] Where to look
[   84.707]   journalctl -b -u immucore
[   84.708]   /run/immucore/
